I'm continuing the config.go work. I have config.go and config_test.go in context now (the middle of config_test.go was truncated around lines 173-286 but I saw the key tests; the truncated region has TestNetBracketsOutsideQuotes continuation and probably parseConfigBlocks tests — I have enough).

Now make the config.go edits:

1. parseKeyValue: strip trailing comment.
2. dropEmptyManagedGroups: adjacency rule.
3. upsertHookEntries force path: call dropEmptyManagedGroups.

Edit 1 — parseKeyValue:

```go
// parseKeyValue parses a single `key = value` TOML line, decoding basic and
// literal strings. Booleans and numbers come back raw. An unquoted trailing
// comment is stripped first: entries carrying comments like
// `type = "command" # entire` must still be recognized as ours, and a
// commented command line must decode to the exact command so idempotent
// installs leave it (and the comment) untouched.
func parseKeyValue(line string) (key string, value string, ok bool) {
	trimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)
	if trimmed == "" || strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, "#") {
		return "", "", false
	}
	eq := strings.Index(trimmed, "=")
	if eq < 0 {
		return "", "", false
	}
	key = strings.TrimSpace(trimmed[:eq])
	value = strings.TrimSpace(stripTrailingComment(strings.TrimSpace(trimmed[eq+1:])))
	...
}
```

And add stripTrailingComment helper after parseKeyValue (before unescapeBasicString):

```go
// stripTrailingComment removes an unquoted TOML comment from a value: the
// first '#' outside any quoted string ends the value. Quoted '#' characters
// survive, and a value that never closes its quote keeps the '#' — malformed
// input is left as-is rather than guessed at.
func stripTrailingComment(value string) string {
	var quote byte
	for i := 0; i < len(value); i++ {
		ch := value[i]
		switch {
		case quote != 0:
			if ch == '\\' && quote == '"' && i+1 < len(value) {
				i++ // skip the escaped character
				continue
			}
			if ch == quote {
				quote = 0
			}
		case ch == '"' || ch == '\'':
			quote = ch
		case ch == '#':
			return strings.TrimRight(value[:i], " \t")
		}
	}
	return value
}
```

Hmm — wait, one edge: a single-quoted literal string in TOML can contain... no escapes in literal strings, so `\` handling only for basic strings — my switch handles quote == '"' only for escapes. Correct.

Edge: value = `"a\"b" # c` — scan: '"' opens quote; 'a'; '\' escape skip; '"'; 'b'; '"' closes; ' '; '#' outside → truncate → `"a\"b"` → decode → `a"b` ✓.

Edge: unclosed quote `command = "oops # not a comment` → quote stays open at '#' (inside quote case → no truncation) → value unchanged → the quote-stripping in parseKeyValue checks first/last char: value[0]=='"' and last char is 't'? `"oops # not a comment` — last char 't' not '"' → raw. Fine, malformed input.

Edit 2 — dropEmptyManagedGroups:

```go
// dropEmptyManagedGroups removes [[hooks.<Event>]] group headers for our
// managed events that no longer parent any hook entry: an empty group is
// kept only when a same-event [[hooks.<Event>.hooks]] entry immediately
// follows it, which is the parent relationship TOML array-of-tables gives
// an entry. A group that still carries content (a matcher) always stays,
// and foreign events are never touched.
func dropEmptyManagedGroups(blocks []tomlBlock) []tomlBlock {
	out := make([]tomlBlock, 0, len(blocks))
	for i, block := range blocks {
		event, kind := headerEvent(block.header)
		if event != "" && kind == "group" && isManagedEvent(event) &&
			len(block.body) == 0 && !followedBySameEventEntry(blocks, i) {
			continue
		}
		out = append(out, block)
	}
	return out
}

// followedBySameEventEntry reports whether the block after blocks[i] is a
// hook entry of the same event: parseConfigBlocks starts every header its
// own block, so the next block is exactly where an entry's parent group
// ends.
func followedBySameEventEntry(blocks []tomlBlock, groupIndex int) bool {
	if groupIndex+1 >= len(blocks) {
		return false
	}
	event, kind := headerEvent(blocks[groupIndex+1].header)
	groupEvent, _ := headerEvent(blocks[groupIndex].header)
	return event == groupEvent && kind == kindHooks
}
```

Wait — check existing behavior compatibility with TestUninstallHooks_RemovesEmptyManagedGroups: install into empty config → our 5 events each have group+entry. Uninstall: dropEntireEntries removes entries; blocks now have 5 empty managed groups; dropEmptyManagedGroups with adjacency: each group's next block is the NEXT group header (not an entry) or EOF → all dropped ✓. Test asserts no "hooks." remains ✓.

TestUninstallHooks_RemovesOnlyEntireEntries: user's group `[[hooks.SessionStart]]` empty body + user's entry. Install: upsertOne inserts our group+entry after user's entry. Uninstall: drop our entry; blocks: user group (empty body!) + user entry + our group (empty, ours). Adjacency: user group followed by user entry (same event, kindHooks) → KEPT ✓. Our group followed by EOF → dropped ✓. Content keeps "[[hooks.SessionStart]]" ✓ and `command = "my-own-start-hook"` ✓.

TestRepairParentlessHooks_SingleGroupForAllEntries: repair inserts synthetic group immediately before the first parentless entry → adjacency OK if dropEmpty were called (it isn't in that path). Fine.

Now — one important regression risk: the PREVIOUS dropEmptyManagedGroups used a global eventsWithEntries check. With adjacency, consider user config where their group has a matcher body + their entry, and separately OUR group+entry was inserted... normal. What about a user group with matcher body but NO entries at all (user authored, empty of entries but non-empty body)? My new rule: `len(block.body) == 0` required to drop — matcher body → kept ✓ (same as before).

What about an empty managed group followed by an entry with a DIFFERENT event (cross-event weirdness)? `[[hooks.SessionStart]]` followed by `[[hooks.Stop.hooks]]` — not same-event → the SessionStart group gets dropped on uninstall/force. Hmm — is that right? TOML-wise the SessionStart group is parentless of entries — dropping is consistent with previous behavior? Previous behavior: eventsWithEntries[SessionStart] — if no SessionStart entries exist anywhere, dropped too. If SessionStart entries exist LATER in the file (non-adjacent)... e.g.:

```toml
[[hooks.SessionStart]]            # empty, user's, followed by...
[[hooks.SessionStart.hooks]] entry  ← NOT adjacent! Wait, it IS adjacent here.
```

Non-adjacent case:
```toml
[[hooks.SessionStart]]
matcher = "..."        ← non-empty body → kept regardless
```

Non-adjacent empty group with entries elsewhere:
```toml
[[hooks.SessionStart]]              # empty group A
[[hooks.SessionStart]]              # empty group B
[[hooks.SessionStart.hooks]] entry  # parents group B
