The test `TestUninstallHooks_KeepsUserAuthoredPreCompactGroup` failed: uninstall REMOVED the user's own empty PreCompact group. Why?

Trace: the test writes a config with ONLY `[[hooks.PreCompact]]` (empty group, no entry). Then install (four entries) appends our groups+entries. Then removeHookEntries:
- dropEntireEntries removes our four entries.
- dropEmptyManagedGroups with isCleanedEvent: PreCompact IS in legacyManagedEvents → the user's empty PreCompact group gets dropped!

Right — my test asserted the conservative behavior ("a user-authored empty PreCompact group is theirs, keep it"), but my chosen implementation (option (a): legacy list) drops ANY empty PreCompact group on uninstall, including user-authored ones.

I noted this tradeoff when designing: option (a) drops any empty group for an event we (ever) managed; option (b) only drops groups that actually parented our removed entries. When I designed the test I wrote the (b) expectation, but implemented (a). Contradiction to resolve.

Which behavior is right? Consider the semantics I established for MANAGED events earlier today: an empty managed group is dropped on uninstall/force UNLESS followed by a same-event entry. That already drops user-authored empty `[[hooks.Stop]]` groups (no entries anywhere). I accepted that: "Force semantics: an empty group with no entries does nothing functionally." So for consistency, the (a) behavior — dropping user-authored empty PreCompact groups on uninstall — is the same established semantics, just extended to a legacy event.

But is it acceptable for a LEGACY event? The user never chose PreCompact; an empty PreCompact group in a config is overwhelmingly likely to be OUR residue (we installed it for months). The risk of deleting a user's intentionally-empty PreCompact group is small, and the group does nothing (empty matcher group). Hmm.

Two ways to resolve:
1. Keep implementation (a), fix the TEST to expect removal (assert the group is dropped as residue).
2. Switch to implementation (b) — only drop empty groups whose event lost an owned entry in this pass — and keep the test as written.

Implementation (b) is more precise and defensible: "we clean up exactly what we installed." It also handles the real migration scenario perfectly (our entry WAS under that group). And it avoids ever deleting user content we didn't create. The cost: dropEntireEntries must report which events lost owned entries, and the two callers pass that set into dropEmptyManagedGroups.

For MANAGED events, (b) would change today's behavior: an empty `[[hooks.Stop]]` group with no owned entry anywhere would no longer be dropped... wait, no — with (b), the Stop group from OUR install did parent our Stop entry (which gets dropped) → in the removed set → dropped. The user-authored empty Stop group with NO owned entry: under (b) it would be KEPT. Under today's shipped behavior (a-for-managed), it's DROPPED. Hmm — changing that alters behavior I shipped an hour ago and wrote tests for (TestUninstallHooks_RemovesOnlyEntireEntries asserts "[[hooks.SessionStart]]" remains — wait, in that test the group has the user's ENTRY following it (adjacency) so it's kept under both). TestUninstallHooks_RemovesEmptyManagedGroups: config built by OUR install → groups parented our entries → dropped under both. So (b) doesn't break the existing tests; it only changes the untested edge (user-authored empty managed group with no entries at all).

Which do I prefer conceptually? (b): "remove exactly our footprint; leave content we didn't write." A user-authored empty `[[hooks.Stop]]` group is content we didn't write; deleting it was arguably over-aggressive anyway. Hmm — but it IS an event we manage; on force-install we then re-create our own group... leaving their empty group is harmless.

BUT — (b) has a wrinkle for the FORCE path: force = dropEntireEntries + dropEmptyManagedGroups + re-insert. Under (b), empty managed groups with no removed entries (user-authored) survive force — fine.

And there's a subtle (b) benefit for the legacy case: precise — only configs that actually carry our owned entry get the PreCompact group cleaned. The user-authored empty PreCompact group test passes.

However — (b) requires threading state: dropEntireEntries returns (kept, removedEvents); removeHookEntries and upsertHookEntries-force pass removedEvents to dropEmptyManagedGroups(blocks, removedEvents). dropEmptyManagedGroups then drops an empty group iff (a) it's a managed event (today's installs) OR its event lost an owned entry — with the adjacency guard. Actually with (b), do we even need the isManagedEvent check? If an event lost an owned entry, the group that parented it should be dropped when empty. If a managed event's group is empty but NO owned entry was dropped from anywhere (user-authored), (b) keeps it. So the predicate becomes just `removedEvents[event]` — the isManagedEvent check becomes unnecessary! Cleaner: dropEmptyManagedGroups(blocks, removedEvents) drops empty groups for events in removedEvents (plus adjacency guard).

Wait, but check TestUninstallHooks_RemovesEmptyManagedGroups: our install created group+entry per event; uninstall drops entries (removedEvents = all four) then groups → dropped ✓. ForeignEntryKeepsOnlyItsGroup: user group empty-body followed by user entry → adjacency keeps ✓; our group → dropped ✓. Legacy test: our old entry under PreCompact → removedEvents[PreCompact]=true → empty group dropped ✓. KeepsUserAuthoredPreCompactGroup: no owned PreCompact entry → not in removedEvents → kept ✓. All consistent!

And FORCE path: dropEntireEntries gives removedEvents (our entries) → dropEmptyManagedGroups drops their groups → re-insert fresh. The no-accumulation test still passes ✓.

